What is the OAuth 2.0 Device Flow, first?

Imagine you're setting up a smart TV app (like Netflix on your TV). The TV shows a code on screen and says "go to netflix.com/activate on your phone or computer and enter this code." You log in on your phone, and the TV magically gets logged in too.

That's the device flow — it exists for devices that can't easily type a username/password (TVs, IoT devices, printers, etc.). It's a login trick for input-limited devices.

What's the problem Salesforce is fixing?

People started using this "TV login trick" for things it wasn't meant for — like regular apps, scripts, or integrations that could just use normal login flows. That's risky because the device flow was never designed with strong security controls for general-purpose use. It's more exploitable if misused.

So Salesforce is saying: "You can only use this flow if you're actually building something like a TV/IoT app."

How do they enforce "this is really for TV/IoT-like apps"?

They added strict requirements. Your app must be:

An External Client App (a newer app type — not the old "Connected App")
Set to "local" distribution (meaning: not distributed/shared broadly — it's local to your org)
Using localhost as the callback URL (i.e., it's not calling back to some public website — localhost signals it's a locally-run device/process)
Not using any other OAuth login flow at the same time (it can only do device flow, nothing else)

If your app doesn't check all 4 boxes → it's "non-compliant" and will get blocked.

Important callout: Connected Apps (the older app type) are automatically non-compliant, always. If you're using a Connected App with device flow today, you must migrate to an External Client App — there's no way to make an old-style Connected App compliant.

