What is OAuth mean?

Great question — let's build this up from scratch.

The problem OAuth solves

Imagine a third-party app (let's say a scheduling tool) wants to read your Salesforce data. The old, bad way to do this would be: you give the scheduling tool your Salesforce username and password directly. It logs in as you.

That's terrible for a few reasons:

The app now has your actual password — if it gets hacked, so does your Salesforce account.
The app can do anything you can do — no limits.
You can't easily revoke access without changing your password (which breaks everything else too).
What OAuth actually is

OAuth 2.0 is a standard way for you to grant an app limited access to your data, without ever handing over your password.

Think of it like a hotel key card instead of your house key:

You (the guest) don't get the master key.
The front desk (Salesforce) gives you a card (a token) that opens only your room, for a limited time.
If you lose the card, the hotel just deactivates that card — your actual identity/password is untouched.
How it works, simply
You try to use a third-party app that wants to connect to Salesforce.
The app redirects you to Salesforce's own login page — you type your password only into Salesforce, never into the third-party app.
Salesforce asks you: "This app wants to read your contacts. Allow?" — you approve (or deny).
Salesforce gives the app a token (a temporary, limited-permission key) — not your password.
The app uses that token to talk to Salesforce on your behalf, but only within the permissions you approved.
Where "device flow" fits in

There are several ways (called "flows") to get that token, depending on the situation:

Normal login flow — you're on a website/app with a browser, you log in directly.
Device flow — you're on something without a good way to type a password (a TV remote, an IoT device) — so it shows a code, and you approve it from a different device (like your phone) that does have a keyboard/browser.

That's the piece from the article: Salesforce is tightening the rules on who's allowed to use that TV-style device flow, because it started being used in places it wasn't meant for.

