how to check whether my application uses device flow or not?

Good practical question. There are two places to check — Salesforce side (the app configuration) and your code side (how your app actually talks to Salesforce). Let's go through both.

1. Check on the Salesforce side (config)

This tells you whether device flow is enabled for your app, regardless of whether it's currently being used.

If you're using a Connected App:

Go to Setup (gear icon → Setup)
In the Quick Find box, type App Manager
Find your app in the list → click the dropdown arrow → View (or Edit)
Look under OAuth Policies / OAuth Settings section
Check if there's a checkbox like "Enable Device Flow" — if it's checked, device flow is enabled for that app

If you're using an External Client App:

Setup → Quick Find → External Client Apps → External Client App Manager
Open your app → go to the OAuth Settings / Policies section
Same thing — look for the Device Flow toggle/checkbox

Tip: If you're not sure which type your app is, App Manager and External Client App Manager are two separate lists in Setup — check both.

2. Check on the code/integration side (how it's actually being used)

Even if device flow is enabled, your app might not actually be using it. To confirm actual usage, look at your app's authentication code or integration logs for the grant_type parameter sent to Salesforce's token endpoint.

Device flow specifically uses these:

A request to /services/oauth2/token with grant_type=urn:ietf:params:oauth:grant-type:device_code (or a call to a /services/oauth2/device/authorize endpoint first)

If your code/integration doesn't reference device_code or a /device/authorize call anywhere — you're not using device flow, even if it happens to be enabled in settings.

3. Salesforce will also tell you directly (starting soon)

Per the article you shared: starting August 2026, Salesforce will show a banner directly on the app's setup page if device flow is enabled — so if you wait, Salesforce will flag it for you automatically. But checking now is safer than waiting.
